1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:04,493
The brush uncovers a coin, and it is immediately strange.

2
00:00:04,493 --> 00:00:09,502
Indian coins so far have been silver pieces with punched marks and no name.

3
00:00:12,733 --> 00:00:16,491
This one carries a head in profile and lettering around the edge.

4
00:00:16,491 --> 00:00:19,239
Something has arrived from somewhere else.

5
00:00:21,200 --> 00:00:25,756
Texts, including Buddhist and Jain writings that describe rival teachers.

6
00:00:25,756 --> 00:00:30,005
Inscriptions, including Persian royal ones that mention this region.

7
00:00:30,005 --> 00:00:33,145
Coins, which are the clearest evidence of contact.

8
00:00:33,145 --> 00:00:38,617
And Greek accounts of Alexander's campaign, written by people who were not neutral.

9
00:00:43,267 --> 00:00:44,854
This was an age of argument.

10
00:00:44,854 --> 00:00:49,807
Buddhism and Jainism are the two that lasted, but they were part of a crowded field.

11
00:00:52,433 --> 00:00:58,955
The Buddhist texts themselves list rival teachers and take their ideas seriously enough to argue with them.

12
00:00:58,955 --> 00:01:00,643
Two are worth knowing.

13
00:01:04,667 --> 00:01:09,659
The Ajivikas, associated with Makkhali Gosala, held a doctrine of niyati — fate.

14
00:01:09,659 --> 00:01:16,686
Everything is determined, and effort changes nothing; liberation comes in its own time regardless of what you do.

15
00:01:16,686 --> 00:01:23,089
The order lasted for centuries and had royal patronage, including caves granted under the Mauryas.

16
00:01:26,267 --> 00:01:32,509
The Charvakas, also called Lokayata, went the other way entirely: they accepted only what the senses can verify,

17
00:01:32,509 --> 00:01:38,861
rejected an afterlife and the authority of scripture, and argued that pleasure in this life is the reasonable aim.

18
00:01:38,861 --> 00:01:45,655
Almost nothing of their own writing survives — we know them mainly through opponents quoting them in order to refute them,

19
00:01:45,655 --> 00:01:48,883
which is worth remembering when you read about them.

20
00:01:55,233 --> 00:01:59,890
Then the invasions, and the Persians came first — which students routinely forget.

21
00:01:59,890 --> 00:02:05,612
Under Cyrus and then Darius the First, the Achaemenid empire took control of parts of the north-west,

22
00:02:05,612 --> 00:02:09,895
and Persian royal inscriptions name this region among their provinces.

23
00:02:12,633 --> 00:02:16,937
The consequences were lasting: trade with west Asia, the Aramaic script,

24
00:02:16,937 --> 00:02:22,839
from which Kharoshthi develops, and administrative ideas that later Indian states made use of.

25
00:02:24,933 --> 00:02:29,975
Alexander came in 326 BCE. He defeated Porus at the Hydaspes, the Jhelum,

26
00:02:29,975 --> 00:02:38,518
in a battle the Greek sources describe at length and then, according to those same sources, restored him to his kingdom.

27
00:02:40,467 --> 00:02:47,104
He pushed east to the Beas — and there his army refused to go further, having heard what the Nandas could field.

28
00:02:47,104 --> 00:02:49,923
He turned back, and died at Babylon soon after.

29
00:02:49,923 --> 00:02:52,396
He was in India for about two years.

30
00:02:57,600 --> 00:02:59,166
So how much did it change?

31
00:02:59,166 --> 00:03:02,647
One view says very little: Alexander held nothing for long,

32
00:03:02,647 --> 00:03:09,329
no Indian source of the time thinks him worth mentioning, and the state that mattered most was never engaged.

33
00:03:14,067 --> 00:03:20,805
The other says the contact mattered more than the conquest — routes opened, Indo-Greek kingdoms followed in the north-west,

34
00:03:20,805 --> 00:03:25,641
and the political looseness he left behind helped the next Indian empire expand into it.

35
00:03:25,641 --> 00:03:26,782
Both are defensible.

36
00:03:26,782 --> 00:03:32,299
Be careful of accounts on either side that need him to have been either decisive or irrelevant.

37
00:03:38,667 --> 00:03:41,919
Here is the memory tip, carved in stone.

38
00:03:41,919 --> 00:03:43,189
Persians first.

39
00:03:43,189 --> 00:03:44,379
Greeks second.

40
00:03:44,379 --> 00:03:45,948
Neither stayed.

41
00:03:50,400 --> 00:03:51,108
Dig Quiz.

42
00:03:51,108 --> 00:03:52,454
Pause and name it.

43
00:03:52,454 --> 00:03:58,757
First: the school that held everything is determined by fate and effort changes nothing.

44
00:03:58,757 --> 00:04:00,049
The Ajivikas.

45
00:04:02,667 --> 00:04:06,944
Second: the school that accepted only what the senses verify.

46
00:04:06,944 --> 00:04:08,875
The Charvakas, or Lokayata.

47
00:04:08,875 --> 00:04:14,073
Third: the river where Alexander's army refused to march further east.

48
00:04:18,033 --> 00:04:18,727
The Beas.

49
00:04:18,727 --> 00:04:24,854
In Mains, a frequent question asks what the Persian and Greek contacts left behind.

50
00:04:26,800 --> 00:04:31,585
Name the script, the trade, the administrative borrowing and the later Indo-Greek kingdoms,

51
00:04:31,585 --> 00:04:34,746
and be clear about what the evidence will not support.

52
00:04:37,567 --> 00:04:42,204
A coin with a face on it takes its place, and the third room of the gallery is full.

53
00:04:44,633 --> 00:04:50,976
Next gallery: the empire that takes all of this and builds something the subcontinent had never seen.

54
00:04:50,976 --> 00:04:54,821
To catch it when it's out, subscribe to Gyaan-X Studios.

